---
description: Safe checkout rules for workflows that combine the platform and application repositories
globs: .github/{workflows,actions}/**/*.yml
alwaysApply: false
---

# Multi-repository checkout safety

- Treat the GitHub workspace root as owned by the caller repository.
- Every `actions/checkout` with `repository:` must also set a unique `path:`.
- Never assume a called reusable workflow can access local actions or config from
  the repository that defines the workflow. Default checkout retrieves the caller.
- Keep application source in `app-source/`; run build commands there.
- Do not use a second root checkout: it can clean or replace platform files.
- Use manifest/config inputs for repository identity; do not hardcode an unknown
  platform owner or repository.

## Reusable workflows and local scripts

`uses:` is resolved from the workflow file's git tree before any step runs.
`uses: ./.github/actions/...` in a reusable workflow is the caller's tree when
GitHub resolves it against the caller, and `uses: ./platform/.github/actions/...`
is never valid: `platform/` does not exist in that git tree.

A reusable workflow that needs a script, template, or config from this
repository must:

1. Parse the called workflow from `GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF`
   (`owner/repo/.github/workflows/file.yml@ref`). The text before
   `/.github/workflows/` is the repository. The text after `@` is the ref.
   Do not use `github.sha` or `github.repository`; those are the caller.
2. Check that repository out with `actions/checkout` at `path: platform`.
   The token must be able to read that repository. Same-repo callers use
   `github.token`. External callers use `TEMPLATES_TOKEN` or another secret
   declared on the workflow. Set `persist-credentials: false`.
3. Check the caller repository out at the workspace root before the
   `platform/` checkout. A later root checkout runs `git clean` and deletes
   `platform/`.
4. When the caller checkout is a git repository, append `platform/` to
   `.git/info/exclude` so version commits do not include it.
5. Run the script from that checkout, for example
   `bash platform/.github/actions/<name>/<name>.sh`, with the same `env`
   the composite `action.yml` sets. Keep the workflow step `id` so
   `GITHUB_OUTPUT` still maps to `steps.<id>.outputs`.
6. Leave `platform/` in place for the rest of the job. Copy
   `platform/scripts/release_model.py` to `$RUNNER_TEMP` when a script needs
   it. Do not delete the checkout before those scripts run.
7. For a composite action that also runs third-party steps (`setup-node`,
   `upload-artifact`, `download-artifact`), keep those `uses:` lines in the
   workflow and point only the local script at `platform/`.

`uses: ./.github/actions/...` is valid only in a workflow that runs in this
repository (`ops-*.yml`) after that repository is checked out at the workspace
root.

Safe application checkout:

```yaml
- uses: actions/checkout@v7
  with:
    repository: ${{ inputs.repository }}
    ref: ${{ inputs.ref }}
    token: ${{ secrets.PAT }}
    path: app-source
```

Safe platform checkout inside a reusable workflow:

```yaml
- name: Resolve platform checkout
  id: platform_ref
  shell: bash
  run: |
    set -euo pipefail
    ref="${GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF##*@}"
    repo="${GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF%@*}"
    repo="${repo%%/.github/workflows/*}"
    if [[ -z "$ref" || -z "$repo" || "$repo" == "$GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF" ]]; then
      echo "::error::Could not parse the platform repository from GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF."
      exit 1
    fi
    {
      echo "ref=$ref"
      echo "repository=$repo"
    } >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
- name: Checkout platform repository
  uses: actions/checkout@v7
  with:
    repository: ${{ steps.platform_ref.outputs.repository }}
    ref: ${{ steps.platform_ref.outputs.ref }}
    token: ${{ github.token }}
    path: platform
    persist-credentials: false
- name: Run the platform script
  id: example
  shell: bash
  env:
    WORK_DIR: ${{ inputs.working_directory }}
  run: bash platform/.github/actions/example/example.sh
```
